Date: 2026-04-23
Descriptor: attach_only (validates ADR 0014 — and surfaces a
            compound-class pipeline limitation)
Purpose:
    Demonstrate the two-phase discovery/attach workflow on the
    canonical mixed graph with new data added. Verify attach-only
    mode rejects novel canonical forms cleanly. Observe what
    happens to fresh data that *should* attach but doesn't.

Mechanism version:
    ADR 0014 (NamingPolicy.attach_only, SkipReason::NoMatchingPattern).
    Source: v2/src/lib.rs at the commit implementing 0014.
    Runner: v2/examples/attach_only.rs.

Command:
    cargo run --example attach_only

====================================================================
RAW OUTPUT
====================================================================

=== Phase 1 — discovery pass (default policy) ===
  canonical [(0, 0), (0, 0), (0, 0)]  ->  Named(p_0)
  canonical [(1, 0)]  ->  Skipped(BelowMinEdges 1<2)
  canonical [(1, 0), (1, 0), (1, 0)]  ->  Named(p_1)
  canonical [(1, 2), (2, 0)]  ->  Named(p_2)
patterns registered: 3
RSet size: 30

=== Phase 2 — new data arrives ===
  added R(m1, m2)
  added R(m2, m3)
  added R(m3, m1)
  added R(u, v)
  added R(v, w)
  added R(q1, q2)
  added R(q1, q3)
  added R(q3, q4)
RSet size: 38

=== Phase 3 — attach-only pass ===
  canonical [(0, 0), (0, 0), (0, 0)]  ->  Named(p_0)
  canonical [(1, 0)]  ->  Skipped(NoMatchingPattern)
  canonical [(1, 0), (1, 0), (1, 0)]  ->  Skipped(AlreadyKnown)
  canonical [(1, 2), (2, 0)]  ->  Skipped(AlreadyKnown)

patterns registered: 3  (must equal phase-1 count)
total named instances: 4

=== Per-pattern instance counts ===
  p_0  instances = 2
  p_1  instances = 1
  p_2  instances = 1

====================================================================
WHAT LANDED / WHAT DID NOT
====================================================================

Landed:
  - `p_0` (3-cycle) absorbed the new {m1,m2,m3} cycle as a second
    instance. Instance count goes 1 → 2. ADR 0014's intent is
    satisfied for this input.
  - `p_1` (3-star) and `p_2` (2-chain) are AlreadyKnown because the
    original instance's participants re-match on the dedup check.
  - Everything with canonical [(1, 0)] is rejected with
    NoMatchingPattern — attach-only correctly refuses to mint a new
    pattern for single-edge canonical.
  - No new patterns created. Registry count stays at 3. The
    attach-only guarantee is upheld.

**Did NOT land as intended:**

  The fresh 2-chain `{u, v, w}` — structurally isomorphic to p_2 —
  was *not* recorded as a new p_2 instance. After phase 3, p_2
  still has 1 instance. The `{q1, q2, q3, q4}` T-fork, whose
  canonical form `[(2,0), (3,1), (3,2)]` is genuinely novel, was
  also not surfaced as a `NoMatchingPattern` rejection of that
  canonical form — it is silently absorbed into the `[(1, 0)]`
  single-edge bucket.

====================================================================
ROOT CAUSE: COMPOUND-CLASS FRAGMENTATION
====================================================================

The pipeline (`run_naming_pass`) uses `compound_class_subgraphs`,
which groups edges by their compound fingerprint
(endpoint-profile-pair × locality) and then runs connected-
component extraction *per group*. A connected structure can only
materialize as a single subgraph if all its edges share a compound
fingerprint.

The original 2-chain `{c2, c3, c4}` does:
  - c2/c3/c4 all have the same profile post-naming
    (Both-out=1-in=2), so every edge shares the same endpoint-pair
    and locality profile. All edges land in one compound class,
    connected-component extraction yields the 2-chain subgraph.

The *new* 2-chain `{u, v, w}` does NOT:
  - u has LeftOnly-out=1-in=0 (chain head, never named).
  - v has Both-out=1-in=1 (middle, never named).
  - w has RightOnly-out=0-in=1 (tail, never named).
  - R(u,v) endpoint pair is (LeftOnly-1-0, Both-1-1).
  - R(v,w) endpoint pair is (Both-1-1, RightOnly-0-1).
  - DIFFERENT compound fingerprints. They land in different
    compound classes. Each class has one edge. Each connected
    component is a single-edge subgraph. The 2-chain structure is
    fragmented and cannot be detected.

In other words: **cyclic / symmetric structures survive, asymmetric
structures fragment.** The 3-cycle worked because every cycle edge
has identical endpoint profile pairs. The 3-star works for the same
reason (spokes identical from the hub's perspective). The 2-chain
fails because its head-edge, middle-edge, and tail-edge are all
distinct compound fingerprints.

Put differently still: two subgraphs with the same canonical form
will generate matching pattern instances only if (a) both are
cyclic / symmetric, or (b) the new data's endpoint profiles happen
to line up with what compound fingerprints the original data
produces.

This is not a bug in ADR 0014. It is a structural limitation of
using `compound_class_subgraphs` as the enumeration strategy. The
same limitation applies to the discovery pass; discovery simply
happens to name the symmetric patterns that do emerge and misses
the asymmetric ones that don't.

====================================================================
IMPLICATIONS
====================================================================

1. **ADR 0014's attach-only mode works correctly on every pattern
   that is discoverable by the current pipeline.** Anything
   `compound_class_subgraphs` surfaces can be attached or rejected
   by the attach-only flag. The three guarantees — no new patterns,
   instance dedup, NoMatchingPattern for unmatched canonicals — all
   hold within that scope.

2. **A separate ADR is needed to handle asymmetric structures.** The
   right direction is subgraph *matching* rather than subgraph
   *discovery*: given a named pattern's canonical form, enumerate
   connected subgraphs of the right size and check canonical
   equality. That is a distinct mechanism (subgraph homomorphism
   search) and its own tradeoff (combinatorial blowup on large
   graphs, small-graph cheap).

3. **Discovery's miss on the original input already demonstrated
   this.** Default γ on the unfiltered mixed graph named three
   patterns. If the original had contained a second 3-chain whose
   internal nodes had different degrees than `{c2, c3, c4}` (e.g.,
   extra unrelated edges on the middle node), the second chain
   would not have been recognized as an instance. The attach-only
   experiment just makes the limit visible by constructing the
   case deliberately.

====================================================================
DECISION / NEXT
====================================================================

ADR 0014 merges as-is. Its scope is the attach-only policy knob on
`run_naming_pass`; it is correct within the scope.

The fragmentation finding is the important output. A follow-up ADR
(0015, provisionally) should introduce **subgraph matching against
named patterns**:

  - Input: a named pattern's canonical form and the current RSet.
  - Output: all connected subgraphs of the RSet whose canonical
    form equals the pattern's.
  - Strategy (minimum first): enumerate connected subgraphs of
    `|canonical|` edges among the data identifiers, canonicalize
    each, compare. Prune via endpoint-profile sketch filters.
  - Wires into `run_naming_pass` as an alternative enumeration path
    when `attach_only=true` (and potentially also when `false`).

Until that ADR lands, attach-only is honest about its constraint:
it attaches every structure the current pipeline can enumerate,
and no more. The T-fork and asymmetric 2-chain cases should be
understood as waypoints for the matching ADR, not as silent misses.
